//
// What a `&` parameter still refuses once it links a scalar field or element
// (`167-a-ref-parameter-links-a-scalar-field-or-element.loft`):
//
// - a value that names no place — a literal, a call's result, a computed value — because the
//   callee's write would land nowhere;
// - an element of a container the callee GROWS, handed in beside that container
//   (`(B-Ref-Reshape)`'s callee clause: a growth that outgrows the allocation moves every
//   element, so the link would name a slot that is gone).  An element's FIELD is the same
//   hazard, and so is an element of a record's vector field when the callee grows it through
//   the record.
//
// A narrow integer store place (`o.a` of type `u8`) was a fourth refusal here until loft#1567
// lifted it at the bind and at the argument together; it now links, and its cell moved to
// `1567-a-link-to-a-narrow-integer-store-place-reads-and-writes-it.loft`.
//
// Every expected error below is matched exactly once, so a second error for one argument
// fails the file.  Before @PLN167 B1 the three reshape cells were refused as "a literal or
// expression" — the right verdict under the wrong rule — and they must stay refused now that
// the argument itself is admitted.
//
// @falsified-at: 92bdca3d9 — interpret exit 1 -> 0, interpret expectations FAIL/7 -> 0/7 (the suite's verdict, not a count of matches), native exit 1 -> 0, native expectations FAIL/7 -> 0/7 (the suite's verdict, not a count of matches).  Measured when the file carried seven cells; the narrow one left with loft#1567 and the remaining six are unchanged by that
// @falsified-by: tests/falsified/167-a-ref-parameter-refuses-a-place-it-cannot-link.patch — interpret exit 1 -> 0, interpret expectations FAIL/7 -> 0/7 (the suite's verdict, not a count of matches), native exit 1 -> 0, native expectations FAIL/7 -> 0/7 (the suite's verdict, not a count of matches)
// HOLDS: leak, panic and free-refusal are clean on both trees and both backends.
//
// CHANNEL: the expected diagnostics, one per refused argument.
//
// @EXPECT_ERROR: Cannot pass a literal or expression to a '&' parameter
// @EXPECT_ERROR: Cannot pass a literal or expression to a '&' parameter
// @EXPECT_ERROR: Cannot pass a literal or expression to a '&' parameter
// @EXPECT_ERROR: cannot pass both `v` and a reference into it to `grow_bump`
// @EXPECT_ERROR: cannot pass both `v2` and a reference into it to `grow`
// @EXPECT_ERROR: cannot pass both `b` and a reference into it to `grow_bag`

struct E { n: integer }
struct Bag { es: vector<E> }

fn bump(c: &integer) { c = c + 1; }
fn grow_bump(c: &integer, v: &vector<integer>) { v += [9]; c = c + 1; }
fn grow(c: &integer, v: &vector<E>) { v += [E { n: 9 }]; c = c + 1; }
fn grow_bag(c: &integer, b: &Bag) { b.es += [E { n: 9 }]; c = c + 1; }
fn five() -> integer { 5 }

fn main() {
  bump(3);
  bump(five());
  x = 1;
  bump(x + 1);
  v: vector<integer> = [1, 2, 3];
  grow_bump(v[0], v);
  v2: vector<E> = [E { n: 1 }];
  grow(v2[0].n, v2);
  b = Bag { es: [E { n: 1 }] };
  grow_bag(b.es[0].n, b);
}
